\label{prop:monotone_general}
For $K{\geq}3$ under the same conditions, numerical verification across 20{,}000 random $(C_0,p,g)$ triples per $K$ shows worst-case deviations $< 0.005$ from strict monotonicity (Appendix~\ref{app:monotonicity_proof}).
\end{observation}

\begin{conjecture}[$\varepsilon$-approximate envelope monotonicity]
\label{conj:monotone}
For non-IV topologies under $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$, $|B(C(\delta))|$ is monotonic up to $\varepsilon < 0.06$ deviations under default DGP parameters (collider/M-bias non-increasing, others non-decreasing; see Appendix~\ref{sec:conjecture_verify} for DGP-dependent bounds). Verified across 30{,}000 random matrices per topology, $K \in \{3,5,7\}$, $\tau \in \{0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\}$.
\end{conjecture}

Confounding is consistently the most robust topology (highest ASV or no violation), and M-bias remains immune (not shown). However, the relative fragility of exposure vs.\ IV inverts with coefficient sign structure: under alternating signs, exposure is most fragile ($0.090 < 0.203$); under same-sign effects, IV is most fragile ($0.203 < 0.223$). Practitioners should compute ASV with their specific DGP rather than relying on generic rankings.

\subsection{Extended Conjecture Verification}
\label{sec:conjecture_verify}

We test envelope monotonicity (Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}) by sampling 10{,}000 random $C_0$ matrices from Dirichlet$(1,\dots,1)$ per topology for $K \in \{3,5,7\}$ (210{,}000 total), sweeping $\delta$ in steps of 0.01, and counting matrices with at least one non-monotonic step exceeding tolerance $10^{-12}$. Bias at each $\delta$ is computed via analytic probability limits using Gauss--Hermite quadrature (100 points), eliminating sampling noise entirely.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Envelope monotonicity violations per 10{,}000 random $C_0$ matrices. ``Worst mag.''\ is the largest single-step deviation $|B(\delta_{i+1})|-|B(\delta_i)|$ in the non-monotonic direction.}
\label{tab:conjecture_verify}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Topology} & $K{=}3$ & $K{=}5$ & $K{=}7$ & \textbf{Worst mag.} & \textbf{Direction} \\
\midrule
Confounding & 8467 & 8063 & 7892 & 0.011 & non-dec \\
Mediation   & 8236 & 7905 & 7893 & 0.013 & non-dec \\
Collider    & 8683 & 7669 & 7570 & 0.005 & non-inc \\
Exposure    & 485  & 205  & 77   & 0.056 & non-dec \\
M-bias      & 9791 & 7783 & 7399 & 0.002 & non-inc \\
IV          & 7433 & 6015 & 5711 & $>10^6$ & non-dec \\
Front-door  & 8429 & 8079 & 7899 & 0.013 & non-dec \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Violation counts are high (60--97\% of matrices), but magnitudes are negligible for non-IV topologies: all worst-case deviations fall below 0.06 ($<$6\% of the respective $\tau$). These results are computed from analytic probability limits with zero sampling noise, confirming the violations are structural properties of multi-class confusion matrices rather than numerical artifacts. IV deviations reflect Wald estimator singularities when the first-stage coefficient passes near zero---this is a property of the Wald estimator, not of the ASV parameterization. Adversarial search (500 restarts per configuration, $K \in \{3,5\}$) finds genuine counterexamples for all topologies except IV $K{=}3$, confirming that strict monotonicity does not hold universally. The bias envelope is $\varepsilon$-approximately monotonic with $\varepsilon < 0.03$ for collider and M-bias across all tested $\tau$; confounding, mediation, and front-door achieve $\varepsilon < 0.06$ at default DGP parameters (see DGP sensitivity below); exposure satisfies $\varepsilon_{p99} < 0.008$ but extreme-tail matrices ($<$0.02\% of draws) reach $\varepsilon \approx 0.15$. Empirically, $\varepsilon$ scales as $O(1/K^\alpha)$ with $\alpha \in [0.6, 1.5]$ (topology-dependent): for confounding, mediation, and front-door, $\alpha \approx 1.4$; for exposure, $\alpha \approx 1.0$; for collider and M-bias, $\alpha \approx 0.7$. A universal empirical upper bound is $\varepsilon \leq 0.137/\sqrt{K}$, verified across all tested configurations ($K \in \{3,5,7\}$). This scaling supports the practical validity of ASV as a sensitivity tool at any meaningful bias threshold.

\paragraph{DGP parameter sensitivity.}

The proof (Appendix~\ref{app:monotonicity_proof},~\ref{par:med_monotone}) exploits perspective function convexity; $(C_0)_{jj}{\geq}\tfrac{1}{2}$ excludes degenerate matrices. Proportional effects ($f = \lambda g$) holds when a single latent factor drives both paths---a natural assumption in CSS annotation tasks, where a single quality dimension typically governs both the treatment--covariate and mediator--outcome relationships. Under NLP-realistic DGP conditions, the out-of-bound rate drops to 0.07\% with zero sign failures (Table~\ref{tab:proportional_effects}); even under non-proportional effects with 23 real LLM matrices, sign preservation remains ${\geq}97.3$\% (Table~\ref{tab:proportional_empirical}). Failures concentrate in extreme asymmetric DGPs (Sparse: 24.2\% OOB) unlikely in balanced NLP classification. When $\lambda(C) \notin [0,1]$, Proposition~\ref{prop:conf_gen} provides a complementary bound ($|\tau| > \bar{B}$).

\begin{observation}[Envelope monotonicity---$K{\geq}3$]
\label{prop:monotone_general}
For $K{\geq}3$ under the same conditions, numerical verification across 20{,}000 random $(C_0,p,g)$ triples per $K$ shows worst-case deviations are negligible ($\leq 0.013$; Table~\ref{tab:conjecture_verify} in Appendix~\ref{sec:conjecture_verify}).
\end{observation}

\begin{conjecture}[$\varepsilon$-approximate envelope monotonicity]
\label{conj:monotone}
For non-IV topologies under $C(\delta) = (1{-}\delta)I + \delta C_0$, $|B(C(\delta))|$ is monotonic up to $\varepsilon < 0.06$ deviations under default DGP parameters (collider/M-bias non-increasing, others non-decreasing; see Appendix~\ref{sec:conjecture_verify} for DGP-dependent bounds). Verified across 10{,}000 random matrices per topology for each $K \in \{3,5,7\}$ (210{,}000 total), $\tau \in \{0.1, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0, 5.0\}$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{remark}[Structural distinctions from E-values]
\label{rem:evalue}
ASV diagnostics differ from E-values~\cite{vanderweele_ding2017} in three respects (Table~\ref{tab:sensitivity_comparison}, Appendix):
\emph{(i)~Topology dependence}: the same $C$ preserves sign under confounding but can reverse it under exposure (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}), whereas E-values treat all confounding identically.
\emph{(ii)~Sign-flip diagnostic}: E-values diagnose magnitude robustness but not sign reversal; the sign-flip ASV $\delta^*_{\mathrm{sf}}$ (Algorithm~\ref{alg:correction}) has no E-value counterpart.
\emph{(iii)~Parametric structure}: ASV reduces $K^2$-dimensional confusion matrix space to one dimension via $C(\delta)$, but $B(\delta)$ is topology-specific and can be non-monotone (IV).
\end{remark}

For $K{=}2$, Theorem~\ref{thm:monotone} guarantees exact monotonicity; for $K{\geq}3$, monotonicity rests on numerical evidence (Conjecture~\ref{conj:monotone}), with ASV thresholds robust to $\varepsilon$-approximate deviations ($\varepsilon < 0.06$ under default DGP; Appendix~\ref{sec:conjecture_verify}). Sign-flip ASV $< 1$ flags sign-reversal-prone pairs; magnitude ASV $< \delta_{\text{observed}}$ indicates bias exceeds tolerance (Algorithm~\ref{alg:correction}). Rankings are stable across DGP parameterizations (Appendix~\ref{sec:asv_stability}); binarization introduces additional topology-dependent bias (Appendix~\ref{app:binarization}).

